WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
in today's video we talk about  thermobaric weapons and their effects on  humans but i make a premise i'm not an  expert and i can't replace those who  have made the study of certain topics a  profession but i can still make myself  useful translate into simple words where  possible complex concepts let's start on  march 1st 2022 a senior u.s defense  official tells journalists the  deployment without specifying the exact  location of tos-1 weapon systems capable  of sending thermobaric rockets against  ukrainian targets a dramatic signal  dictated by the russian need to close  the game as quickly as possible after  the ukrainians have demonstrated a great  ability to resist to a more numerous and  better equipped enemy then the visual  confirmation on the web are circulating  the first videos and images that show  some russian convoys carrying these  little weapons but what exactly is a  thermobaric weapon and on what occasions  has it been used throughout history it's  not easy to give a definition above all  because the term has been improperly  used to include in a single category  different weapon systems and to describe  different effects etymology comes to a  rescue the greek words thermos and varus  respectively heat and pressure describe  well the effects of these weapons very  high temperature and dynamic  overpressure or shockwave in the  simplest terms a thermobaric munition is  a type of enhanced explosive munition  that produces the typical effect of a  conventional high temperature explosion  but by consuming the oxygen present in  the air creates a much greater shock  wave thus increasing the lethality zone  and going to hit victims who are located  further away from the epicenter of the  detonation i open and close the  parenthesis thermobaric weapon and fuel  air weapon are not synonymous they are  two species of the same genre enhanced  blast weapons because their operating  principle is different but the effects  are the same a typical thermobaric  device consists of a container of a fuel  mixture that houses a small explosive  burst to charge called a dispersal  charge after the mixture is dispersed as  a cloud and mixes with the surrounding  oxygen the charge causes it to ignite  creating a larger shock wave that does  enormous damage literally crashing  personnel in the immediate vicinity to  death according to a 1993 study by the  u.s defense intelligence agency the  methods of killing against the living  targets are diverse and brutal what  kills is obviously the pressure wave and  subsequent vacuum of oxygen which  collapses the lungs but the victims will  also be severely burned and likely to  breathe in the burning toxic fuel the  same agency in another paper speculates  that because the shock and pressure  waves cause minimal damage to brain  tissue it's possible that victims are  not rendered unconscious by the blast  but instead suffer for several seconds  or minutes while they suffocate but it  doesn't end there outside the area of  the cloud the shock wave travels very  fast and can hit even those who are  beyond the range of the explosion  causing them serious injuries to  internal organs think that the pressure  that is created is up to two times  greater than that generated by a  conventional explosive weapon this is  confirmed by a study of the central  intelligence agency of the united states  for which the effect of such an  explosion in confined spaces is immense  those near the ignition point are  obliterated those at the fringe are  likely to suffer many internal and thus  invisible injuries in addition to  collapsing lungs there is talk of  embolisms in blood vessels  bleeding and lacerations that can lead  to peritonitis ruptured ear drums and  displacement of ice from their sockets  it's not an exaggeration to say that  such devices can have the effects of a  tactical nuclear weapon without the  disadvantage of producing radiations  because of the mixture's ability to  easily creep into any cavity there is  nothing that can protect against its  devastating effects neither natural  terrain features nor field  fortifications that are intermedically  sealed indeed the ideal use is precisely  against enclosed structures such as  bunkers or buildings since the cloud by  sucking in materials nearby to fill the  oxygen void amplifies the destruction of  the structure's load-bearing elements as  reported by a russian military scientist  writing for the military magazine by  anya thermobaric weapons are also  effective in eliminating minefields and  completely destroying the vegetation of  a certain area to make room for  helicopter landing sites in fact the us  first experimented with this system in  the 1960s in vietnam with the famous and  massive daisy cutter bomb capable of  clearing entire sections of forest in  general the category of enhanced  explosive weapons has found use on  several occasions the us used them in  1991 during the first gulf war then in  2001 in afghanistan in an attempt to  flush out al qaeda members sheltering in  the mountains of torabora and more  recently in 2017 against isil members in  the afghan region of nangarhar the  russians on the other hand tested  several thermobaric systems in 1969  during the border conflict with china  then it was the turn of attacks on the  afghan panchi valley in the early 1980s  against massoud's forces and more  recently as interfax reported between  october and december 1999 during the  offensive against the chechen forces in  the direction of the capital grozny  during the russian advance to take the  city two main thermobaric launch systems  were used the devastating toss one also  called buratino which we hear so much  about these days operational since the  early 80s during the soviet afghan war  and developed by the transmasch design  office enomsk it's a 220 millimeters  multiple rocket launcher system mounted  on a t-72 tank chassis its maximum  effective range is 3.5 kilometers but  other sources say it could reach 5  kilometers the other afghanistan veteran  much smaller in size clearly is the  single shot rpoh smell shoulder mounted  launcher with the claimed maximum range  of one kilometer in the maximum  effective range of 600 meters this type  of weapon is at its best when used as a  bunker buster and loses some  effectiveness when used against  dispersed troops in the open field  normally killing no more than a couple  in bursts when used against the armored  vehicles however it usually damages the  vehicle but the crew survives and is  able to return fire to conclude it  remains to be understood if there are  international treaties that prohibit or  limit the production or use of such  weapons unfortunately not but this  doesn't mean that there are no arguments  against their development as reported by  human rights watch since the enhanced  blast weapons cover a very large area  they are prone to indiscriminate use  especially in or near populated areas in  urban settings it's very difficult to  direct the effect of these weapons only  to militaries without involving  civilians because of their very nature  and then for the fact that covering  every corner or crevice make it  virtually impossible for civilians to  take shelter from their destructive  effect so even if a thermobaric weapon  is aimed at a military target its  extensive and uncontrollable effect  doesn't exclude the involvement of  civilian victims and these that is the  use of weapons in attacks that can't  distinguish between militaries and  civilians is prohibited by the geneva  conventions of 1899 and 1907. however  efforts have not yet produced concrete  results given that the 1980 convention  prohibiting the production of certain  conventional weapons deals with  incendiary weapons in which category  however thermobaric weapons would not  fall and if we look at the current  conflict between russia and ukraine the  problem doesn't only concern the  possible use of these weapons but also  of the so-called cluster munitions and  obviously of the fear the nuclear  warheads some suspect that russia has  already used the former despite the  existence of an international convention  of 2008 that prohibits them and that  moscow although not a signatory has  always respected the possible use of the  russian nuclear arsenal instead which  now counts almost 6 000 warheads is  certainly the most worrying given the  recent threats of president putin to use  them if any power should interfere  militarily well we are done for today i  thank you all for your attention and see  you in the next video ciao

